{#
  INTERNAL, NOINDEX, AND DELETABLE ONCE THE CALL IS MADE.

  A2 was built and then held back because five rooms appeared to have no dish
  photography. That was an artifact of photos.json's filename rule, which was
  amended on 2026-09-15, so the constraint is gone and the two treatments can
  be compared on their merits.

  The two rooms here are the ones that test it hardest, from opposite ends.
  Crown Point has 95% photo coverage across 21 dishes and is the best case for
  A2. Mercury has 47% across 47 dishes and is the worst: its sushi and monthly
  specials are almost entirely unphotographed, which is where a photo-per-dish
  layout has to prove it degrades.

  Both render from the same menu.json rows through the same component. The only
  difference is `variant`.
#}
{%- set spriteIcons = ["chopsticks-noodles", "naruto", "ramen-bowl"] %}
{%- set spriteIcons = spriteIcons | withMenuIcons(menuConcepts) %}
{% include "components/icon-sprite.njk" %}
{% include "components/menu-marks.njk" %}

<section class="sect sect--rule" aria-labelledby="page-title">
  <div class="sect__inner">
    <header class="sect__head">
      <h1 class="sect__title" id="page-title">A2 against <em>B2.</em></h1>
      <p class="sect__note">
        Internal. The same rows, the same component, two treatments. Crown Point is the best case
        for photography at 95% coverage; Mercury is the worst at 47%, and its sushi and specials
        are where a photo per dish has to show it degrades.
      </p>
    </header>
  </div>
</section>

{%- for room in [{ id: "crown-point", label: "Crown Point, 21 dishes, 95% photographed" }, { id: "mercury", label: "Mercury, 47 dishes, 47% photographed" }] %}
